index fdd5119..fb3ca35 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -3,6 +3,14 @@
 All notable changes to rctd-py are documented here.
 The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).
 
+## [0.4.4] — 2026-11-06
+
+rctd-py 0.4.4 fixes GPU out-of-memory errors in doublet and multi mode with the Python API's default `batch_size="auto"`, found while answering [#49](https://github.com/p-gueguen/rctd-py/issues/49) ([#50](https://github.com/p-gueguen/rctd-py/pull/50)). `rctd run` is unchanged.
+
+### Fixed
+- **Doublet and multi mode could run out of GPU memory with the default `batch_size="auto"`** (Python API; `rctd run` defaults to `--batch-size 10000` and was not affected). The automatic batch size was computed from full mode's memory per pixel, but in doublet mode a batch row is a (pixel, type, type) triple and in multi mode a candidate type set, each with its own design matrix, and it ignored the count matrix these modes keep on the GPU. On a 44 GB L40S, doublet and multi mode on 39k Visium HD segmented cells (4,747 genes, 40 types, float64) got batches of 200,000 rows and failed. The batch size is now computed per mode from peak memory measured up to 25,000 genes and 150 cell types, after subtracting the memory that does not grow with the batch: the resident count matrix (not in full mode with `stream_gpu=True`) and the genes x types² table of the full-mode fit. It now reads the free memory of the GPU the run uses (`config.device`) and returns the default on CPU. An unknown `mode` now raises, and a warning says when the count matrix or the minimum batch of 1,024 rows does not fit.
+- Because `"auto"` now picks different batch sizes in every mode, weights from `batch_size="auto"` can differ from 0.4.3 in the last digits, as with any change of batch size. On the Visium HD run above, the spot classes and cell types were identical.
+
 ## [0.4.3] — 2026-11-02
 
 rctd-py 0.4.3 adds a scverse-style interface: `rctd.pp.reference` and `rctd.tl.deconvolve`, with `layer` and `key_added`. Results are unchanged: `rctd run` writes the same output as 0.4.2, and `rctd.deconvolve` with its 0.4.2 arguments writes the same keys and values.
